The '''Bartay Society''' was a Burawi religious order founded in 1611 with the approval of the head of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. It functioned as a fraternal order and was modelled in part on the institutional methods of the Volisanian Jesuits. The society was established to support evangelisation, education, and charity within the Burawi sphere.

The Bartay Society developed an extensive institutional presence in [[Burawa]], [[Galwa]], [[Bindorama]], and [[Lacashe]]. Its members founded schools and charitable establishments and became an important part of the educational infrastructure of the late colonial period. In some areas, the society's schools later adopted more liberal teaching methods than those used by older clerical institutions.

The society remained tied to the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] and retained influence in education long after its founding. Its role in schooling and social welfare made it one of the most significant religious institutions of colonial Burawa.

